The question asks what shares a border with the city where a person worked who traveled to California (where Atwell Mill Grove is located) during the gold rush.

The existing summary’s key surviving clue was that Mill City, Butte County, California, was adjacent to and shared a post office with Magalia/Dogtown, possibly linking the relevant area to a bordering entity. Background facts include: the California Gold Rush began after gold discovery was confirmed in March 1848; the Mother Lode was discovered in the early 1850s and stretched about 190 km between Georgetown and Mormon Bar; and Henry Haight managed the Banking House of Page, Bacon & Co. in San Francisco during the gold rush. Atwell Mill Grove is in California.

Newly retrieved information concerns border locations and the Canada–United States border, but none of it identifies the specific city where the gold-rush traveler worked or what shares a border with that city. Relevant new facts include: Cascade City/Cascade was a Canadian Pacific Railway construction–era boom town in the Boundary Country of British Columbia, near the Canada–US border, sometimes called the “Gateway to the Boundary Country”; Virginia, Lempira, Honduras, shares a border with El Salvador;
